// Progression gates (docs/BBQ_SAUCE_PLAN.md §5) — the content that decides
// WHEN a tier of the game becomes available, and what it makes available.
// Data, never hardcoded in sim_core, exactly like `recipes.ron`,
// `machines.ron`, `shop.ron`, `ingredients.ron` and `contracts.ron`; the
// loader is `sim_core::sim::unlocks::UnlockRegistry`.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader rejects a mismatched version rather than silently misreading a
// changed shape, the same way every other data file's loader does.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Until it did, the game gated NOTHING: a new game
// could buy every ingredient and sign every contract in `contracts.ron`,
// so the only thing that grew as you played was the balance. That was fine
// while there was one sauce — there was nothing to hold back. Handing a
// player the whole three-sauce board on their first minute makes the ketchup
// line look like a chore standing between them and the interesting ones. So
// mustard, the second sauce, arrives with an announcement once the ketchup
// line has demonstrably run — and "demonstrably" means it has SOLD KETCHUP,
// by the box. BBQ, the third, arrives the same way off mustard: each sauce
// is earned by the sauce before it, so the tiers are a chain and not a
// stopwatch.
//
// THE ORDER IS KETCHUP, MUSTARD, BBQ since 2026-10-03 (the owner's call; it
// was ketchup, BBQ, mustard). Mustard is the same five-stage shape as the
// ketchup line again, with no cook, so building it is "do what you just did,
// once more" — while BBQ asks the player to treat their own finished ketchup
// as an ingredient, split the run that ships it, and feed a vat two fluids.
// That is the harder idea, and it lands better on a factory that already
// runs two lines. `docs/DECISIONS.md` has the record.
//
// WHAT A GATE GATES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT. An `Unlock` names
// two lists and only two: `ingredients`, the ids removed from the market
// (`data/ingredients.ron`) until it opens, and `contracts`, the ids removed
// from the contracts board (`data/contracts.ron`) until it opens. Both are
// enforced in the simulation — `BuyIngredient` and `SignContract` are
// REFUSED for a locked id, not merely hidden from a panel — so a gate is a
// rule rather than a decoration on the UI.
//
// **NO RECIPE IS EVER LOCKED, and there is no field here to lock one with.**
// The BBQ recipes sit in `data/recipes.ron` from a new game. They are simply
// unbuildable (their raws cannot be bought) and unsellable (nobody is
// asking). That is the whole reason this shape was chosen over an
// `unlocked: bool` on a recipe: a locked recipe would be a state every
// placed machine consults on every tick, on the hottest path in the
// simulation, to answer a question that changes at most a handful of times
// in a session. A gate that is a filter on two lists costs the tick nothing
// at all.
//
// WHY THE GROUP IS NAMED, RATHER THAN EACH ENTRY CARRYING ITS OWN
// `unlock_after`. The announcement is the point of the gate — a gate that
// opens silently is a gate the player never experiences — and an
// announcement needs a subject. "BBQ Sauce unlocked" is a sentence;
// "spice_mix unlocked, molasses unlocked, liquid_smoke unlocked, smokehouse
// trial unlocked, …" is a log. `name` is what the toast says, so it is
// player-facing prose and not an id.
//
// THE TRIGGER IS BOXES OF A NAMED PRODUCT SOLD (the owner's rule,
// 2026-09-26). Each gate names an item and a count: mustard opens after
// 100 boxes of `boxed_ketchup` have been sold, BBQ after 100 boxes of
// `boxed_mustard`. "Sold" is a box handed over as part of a satisfied delivery
// window — the dock being PAID is what selling means in this game
// (`Economy::tick`'s `Paid` event, which now adds each window's mix to
// `lifetime_boxes_sold` box by box). A missed window sells nothing, and a
// lost contract does not take its past sales back. The ledger is lifetime and
// never decreases, so a gate, once open, stays open.
//
// THIS REVERSES WHAT THIS HEADER USED TO ARGUE, and the old argument was
// wrong on its own terms. It said a delivery "is proof that a whole line ran
// end to end and something was shipped" — true, but a delivery window counted
// exactly the same whether it carried one box or eight, so the number it fed
// the gates measured how many windows had elapsed and not how much the
// factory made. Worse, it was blind to WHICH line ran: a player shipping
// nothing but ketchup and a player shipping nothing but BBQ reached the
// mustard gate at the same moment. Boxes of a named product fix both. They
// scale with output, and they make each tier's gate depend on the line before
// it actually running, which is the progression the tiers were drawn as.
//
// FIELDS, per unlock:
//   id           stable content id (the save format does not key on it — see
//                below — but tests and the toast's diff do)
//   name         what the player reads in the announcement
//   after_sold   (item: "<id>", boxes: N) — the product whose sales open this
//                gate, and how many boxes of it. ONE field holding both,
//                because a count without a product, or a product without a
//                count, is not a gate. `item` must be an id something in
//                `data/recipes.ron` PRODUCES *and* that some contract in
//                `data/contracts.ron` ORDERS — only a paid window's `mix`
//                feeds the boxes-sold ledger, so a gate keyed to anything
//                else could never open — and `boxes` must be at least 1,
//                since 0 would be a gate that is open in a new game and
//                therefore not a gate. WHERE EACH IS CAUGHT: `boxes: 0` is
//                refused by the loader itself (`from_ron_str`), so it is a
//                startup panic. The `item` rules are `validate_covers`,
//                which `sim_core`'s test suite runs against these shipped
//                files — a cross-file check is a test here, not a boot-time
//                panic (docs/DECISIONS.md, 2026-09-08 and 2026-09-26). Edit
//                `item` wrongly and `cargo test` fails; the game itself
//                would boot and simply never open the tier.
//   ingredients  `data/ingredients.ron` ids held back until it opens
//   contracts    `data/contracts.ron` ids held back until it opens
//
// At least one of the two lists must be non-empty: an unlock that releases
// nothing is content that does nothing, and the loader rejects it rather
// than shipping a gate with no effect. No id may be gated by two unlocks.
//
// FILE ORDER IS TIER ORDER, and since the trigger changed it is the ONLY
// thing that says so. Two thresholds counting different products are not two
// numbers you can compare — 100 boxes of ketchup is neither more nor less
// than 100 boxes of BBQ — so "the first gate" and "the deepest gate", which
// the Cookbook both needs, are read off the order of the list below. Write a
// new tier after the tier that feeds it.
//
// WHAT IS SAVED. The ledger, and nothing else — `SaveData` stores
// `lifetime_boxes_sold` and derives every gate's state from it against THIS
// file. Saving "bbq_sauce is unlocked" instead would freeze a save against
// the numbers in this file on the day it was written; deriving it means a
// balance pass here re-reads correctly for every existing save.
(
    schema_version: 1,
    unlocks: [
        // THE FIRST GATE IN THE GAME: mustard, after one hundred boxes of
        // ketchup (the owner's rule of 2026-09-26, and the order of
        // 2026-10-03). Until 2026-10-03 this gate opened BBQ instead; the
        // notes below that were measured then say so.
        //
        // ONE HUNDRED BOXES OF KETCHUP: the second sauce is the reward for a
        // ketchup line that has actually SOLD ketchup. A hundred boxes is a
        // line that was built, fed, staffed and then left running — not one
        // that survived a fixed number of windows, which is what the old
        // trigger measured.
        //
        // WHAT IT COSTS IN TIME, measured 2026-09-26 (headless:
        // `reference_chain_with_budget` plus the stocked larder of
        // `sim_core/tests/common`, raws topped back up as a careful player
        // does, at ARCHITECTURE.md §3.9's 30 ticks/sec). It counts ketchup
        // only, so the swap did not move it:
        //
        //   1 order (corner_shop signed  100th box at tick 52 000 — 29 min,
        //   by hand — a new game since    50 delivery windows
        //   2026-09-26 ships NO contract
        //   signed at all, so this is the
        //   cheapest a real run can be)
        //   3 orders signed at tick 0     100th box at tick 33 000 — 18 min,
        //                                 44 windows, 1 630 in the bank
        //
        // WHAT IT OPENS ONTO IS A WHOLE SECOND LINE FROM RAW, so what this
        // gate has to wait for is MONEY: a gate that opens onto a tier the
        // bank cannot afford is an announcement followed by nothing the
        // player can act on. A mustard line out of `data/shop.ron`: 20 belt
        // at 5, 6 pipe at 8, 2 hopper at 40, 1 fluid_tank at 60, crusher 75,
        // mixer 100, bottler 120, packer 90, dock 70 = **743** — the
        // ketchup line's 959 less its vat and that vat's pipe run, since
        // mustard is not cooked (673 for a player who reuses a dock). The
        // three-order row above banks 1 630 at the gate, twice that.
        //
        // THE 2026-09-26 AFFORDABILITY SWEEP (in delivery windows, when this
        // tier was third and cost 959 with its vat) found the same shape:
        // with the three starter orders signed the balance grows about 13 per
        // delivery past the ketchup line's start-up, so a second line is
        // affordable from about the fifteenth delivery on; with corner_shop
        // alone it FALLS about 10 per delivery, because a factory serving one
        // 2-box order overproduces into a pool nobody buys from — a
        // contract-board lesson no gate value can fix. The full table is in
        // this file as of 0a4a0ee (`git show 0a4a0ee:data/unlocks.ron`).
        //
        // It is not another issue #55: a mustard line shares nothing with
        // the ketchup line except bought `vinegar`, so building it cannot
        // starve a running line of anything but stock.
        Unlock(
            id: "mustard_sauce",
            name: "Mustard",
            after_sold: (item: "boxed_ketchup", boxes: 100),
            // Mustard's two raws (`data/ingredients.ron`). `vinegar` is NOT
            // here and must not be: the ketchup mixer drinks it from tick 0,
            // so gating it would lock the shipped line out of its own
            // ingredient. That is the one thing this gate cannot hold back,
            // and it is also the thing that makes the tier interesting.
            ingredients: ["mustard_seed", "turmeric"],
            // Every contract that asks for mustard and nothing BBQ, including
            // `deli_chain`, which asks for ketchup as well — a mixed order is
            // still a mustard order, and signing one without a mustard line is
            // signing up to strike out. The orders that want mustard AND BBQ
            // open with BBQ, the later tier of the two.
            contracts: [
                "pretzel_cart", "deli_counter", "hot_dog_stand", "ballpark_vendor",
                "deli_chain", "sandwich_franchise", "charcuterie_supplier",
            ],
        ),

        // THE SECOND GATE: BBQ, after one hundred boxes of mustard — the
        // same sentence one tier down. It holds back a BRANCH: four recipes
        // that hang off the ketchup line the player already owns, so what it
        // waits for is understanding more than money (the branch is 872).
        //
        // WHAT IT COSTS IN TIME, measured from a NEW GAME on 2026-10-03 by
        // `godot_project/measure_gate_ticks.gd` (fully staffed, raws topped
        // up and money no object — a floor, not a typical run):
        //
        //   MEASUREMENT PENDING
        //
        // Under the old order the second gate (then mustard's, opened by 100
        // boxes of BBQ) landed at tick 125 000, over an hour in, because the
        // BBQ branch's 600-tick cook put only about 1.6 boxes per 1 000 ticks
        // into paid windows. The mustard line has no cook and ships a case
        // per 200 ticks, which is why this order reaches the third sauce
        // sooner. Whoever wants it sooner still has three content-side levers
        // and no Rust to write: a smaller `boxes` here, more mustard demand in
        // `data/contracts.ron`, or a faster mustard recipe.
        //
        // ISSUE #55 IS THIS GATE'S. Building the BBQ branch starves the
        // ketchup line while two new pipe runs and a vat prime — several
        // missed windows against three-strike contracts, so the reward for
        // building the thing the game just unlocked can be losing the order
        // that paid for it. The 2026-09-20 sweep (measured in delivery
        // windows, when BBQ was the first gate; full record in
        // `docs/DECISIONS.md`, "The bank at the moment of building decides
        // whether the BBQ branch costs you the board") found the deciding
        // variable is the BANK of finished boxed_ketchup at the moment of
        // building: the branch absorbs roughly 35 boxes of throughput over
        // about 8 500 ticks, and a factory forty windows in had banked
        // enough to lose nothing. A factory that has sold a hundred boxes of
        // ketchup AND a hundred of mustard is far past that. #55 stays open
        // anyway — its real fixes are its own (#92, #93), outside this file.
        Unlock(
            id: "bbq_sauce",
            name: "BBQ Sauce",
            after_sold: (item: "boxed_mustard", boxes: 100),
            // The BBQ branch's three raws (`data/ingredients.ron`).
            // Finished ketchup is its fourth input and is NOT here: the
            // factory makes it, so it was never on the market to hold back.
            ingredients: ["spice_mix", "molasses", "liquid_smoke"],
            // Every contract that asks for BBQ, including the mixed orders
            // that ask for ketchup or mustard as well. A mixed order is still
            // a BBQ order — signing one before the branch exists would be
            // signing up to strike out.
            contracts: [
                "rib_shack", "smokehouse_trial", "picnic_caterer", "grill_pub",
                "steakhouse_group", "roadhouse_group", "smokehouse_wholesale",
                "bbq_festival", "county_fair", "food_court", "burger_bar",
                "festival_caterers", "national_condiments",
            ],
        ),
    ],
)
